From 9425acd03e6999e83fee1e5cf68bc5208a6f06b8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ed <edimoldovan@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 12:28:44 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 25/32] phy: apple: t6050-usb2: SuperSpeed host on J714c port 1

A port whose node has the "atc-power" window also gets SuperSpeed. After
the USB 2.0 flip, phy_init() runs the port 1 SuperSpeed host sequence
measured over the m1n1 proxy, steps 1-16:
- the iBoot glue values back in cd28/cd2c/cd40/cd44 (the flip's core soft
  reset clears them; they take writes only with CIO off)
- xHCI reset with CIO off
- controller tunables and the TXDETRX lock request
- the CIO power domains and atc1_phymxwrap through direct PMGR writes,
  then the PHY power control with the PHY reset still asserted
- PHY tunables
- sleep control, crossbar and lane mode (USB3 mode, normal orientation)
- the BIST dance, with the PHY CPU's RTKit firmware booted in between
  (a polling HELLO / EPMAP / power handshake on the MxWrap mailbox) and
  the PHY reset released after UC_TNBL_DONE
- PIPE lock, mux to the USB3 PHY, unlock, TXDETRX release

phy_calibrate(), which usb_add_hcd calls after the xHCI driver's reset,
writes the glue back if the reset changed it and sets host mode. The hub's
USB 2.0 port reset then takes the device to SuperSpeed.

t6050-j714c-usb2.dtsi: usb2phy_1 gets the Type-C PHY, PMGR and controller
windows and the 13 apple,tunable-* properties (the usb-drd1 and atc-phy1
ADT tunables, offsets relative to the window of the same name). Kconfig
selects APPLE_TUNABLE.

Checked on J714c, one boot, booted with pd_ignore_unused: a SanDisk
Extreme Pro in port 1 enumerates as "new SuperSpeed Plus Gen 2x1 USB
device" on usb 4-1 and attaches as sda (1 TB, sda1). Flipped orientation
is not supported.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 16b3737ba9a7b32d220d2c297326e71fec1173a5)
